Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
Hi there! We had this question on our AI&nbsp;
Coffee Break Quiz recently, and we must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.640
discuss its answer here, because we never had&nbsp;
such a contested question before on our Quiz.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.320
So, if you do not know what I’m talking&nbsp;
about here, follow Ms. Coffee Bean to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.160
the Community Tab of this channel,
because we have quite the set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.040
Machine Learning related quiz questions,
and a new question is coming each day!

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.120
But let’s get to our question about&nbsp;
the sequence length of the transformer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.640
It’s not a simple question, not gonna lie.
The answer depends a bit on how one reads it.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.720
Cristian Garcia noted very well&nbsp;
that transformers do not process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.520
just sequences, but something more general:&nbsp;
a sequence without order, which is a set.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.000
The sequential nature of the data becomes&nbsp;
clear to the transformer only because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.600
add positional embeddings to encode the order&nbsp;
in which the data comes in (if it is the case).

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.120
For more about this, check&nbsp;
some of our previous videos.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.560
But supposing we are processing a sequence with&nbsp;
a given model, for example a sequence of words:

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.160
Does the sequence always have to have&nbsp;
the same length, or can this length vary?

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.280
And it’s a valid question, because there are&nbsp;
architectures, like convolutional architectures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.480
with a fully connected layer in the end where&nbsp;
the input must have always the same size.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:21.040
If the image is not the right size, it&nbsp;
gets resized during preprocessing. So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.560
will get to answer this question&nbsp;
for the transformer in two ways:&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.960
First by looking into the code, and&nbsp;
then theoretically, because well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.360
one just does not need to play around a&nbsp;
lot with a transformer from HuggingFace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.840
to notice that inputs which have&nbsp;
different lengths are possible.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.080
So yes, the things we want to&nbsp;
process with a transformer can have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.960
variable lengths. The tokenizer just&nbsp;
gives us more token IDs as output and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.560
an attention mask full of ones, but with&nbsp;
more ones as the sequence grows bigger.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.800
But because of the quadratically scaling&nbsp;
attention in compute, you will also notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.560
parameters in the transformers library limiting&nbsp;
the sequence length, which has the effect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:08.160
truncating everything which exceeds that length&nbsp;
to keep memory and computation in bounds.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.560
Looking at the BERT tokenizer documentation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.800
we also see the pad token and its explanation.&nbsp;
By padding the sequence to a maximum length,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.360
we can produce inputs of fixed&nbsp;
length for the transformer.&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.520
Even the sequence is shorter than the maximum&nbsp;
length, our input length has always the same size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:33.760
because the sequence is padded with the PAD token&nbsp;
and the attention mask is padded with zeros.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.080
And all this trouble is for efficiency&nbsp;
reasons in the implementation when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:44.800
want to train or do inference on more than&nbsp;
one sample at once. Because in that case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.640
tensors in a batch should have the same size&nbsp;
such that the GPU can handle this efficiently.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.080
Therefore, the answer to this question&nbsp;
of whether transformers process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:03:00.640
sequences of a predefined length or a&nbsp;
variable length is both yes and no. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:06.320
sequence length can vary, as in: yes, the&nbsp;
transformer can process any length, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.400
also no, because efficient implementations&nbsp;
fix the length to a maximum length anyway.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.600
But in principle (and I think this is the&nbsp;
most important thing about transformers):&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.840
If everything with positional encodings and&nbsp;
training works well for every sequence length,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:29.840
there is no reason for why transformers should be&nbsp;
bound to a specific set size (or sequence length).

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.280
Because the feed forward layers&nbsp;
we see here are the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.800
no matter the position of&nbsp;
the token we are looking at.&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:43.440
So even if we add here one more token at the&nbsp;
end, this does not care as we take the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.120
feed forward module and its weights&nbsp;
and apply on the newly added tokens.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.480
And the attention is also not caring about the&nbsp;
sequence length as it just computes a pairwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.560
score given a vector and another vector,
so it just looks at vector pairs at a time.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.480
Therefore, Ms. Coffee Bean would&nbsp;
rather say that transformers process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.160
sequences of variable length – in principle.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.840
Okay, bye!

